WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.775
Practical Prophets. Season two. The Book of Judges. Part 2.

00:00:06.575 --> 00:00:32.325
Previously on Practical Prophets. After Joshua's death, Israel struggled to keep the covenant and control the land. Othniel, Ehud, Deborah, and Gideon defeated different oppressors. Gideon's son Abimelech brought civil disaster. Jephthah then negotiated and fought against Ammon, but his victory ended in tragedy at home. Now his victory threatens to turn into another conflict among the Israelites.

00:00:33.425 --> 00:00:36.800
Chapter 12. An Accent That Meant Death.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:46.950
The men of Ephraim confronted Jephthah after his victory. Why had he fought Ammon without them? They threatened to burn his house.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:57.250
Jephthah said he had sought their help and received none. When he saw that no rescue was coming, he had risked his life. Why were they attacking him now?

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:00.300
The answer became civil war.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:15.925
Gilead defeated Ephraim and seized the Jordan crossings. When fleeing men asked to cross, the Gileadites asked if they were Ephraimites. Those who denied it were ordered to pronounce a single word: shibboleth.

00:01:16.225 --> 00:01:28.500
The Ephraimite pronunciation became sibboleth. At the crossings, that sound difference exposed them to death. The chapter gives the number killed as forty-two thousand.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.525
Jephthah judged Israel six years and was buried in one of Gilead's towns.

00:01:34.825 --> 00:02:08.065
Three other judges follow in brief notices. Ibzan of Bethlehem had thirty sons and thirty daughters, arranging marriages outside his household and bringing thirty daughters-in-law into it. He judged seven years and was buried at Bethlehem. Elon of Zebulun judged ten years and was buried at Aijalon. Abdon son of Hillel from Pirathon judged eight years and had forty sons and thirty grandsons riding seventy donkeys. He was buried in Pirathon in Ephraim's hills.

00:02:08.365 --> 00:02:16.090
The ordinary notices of households and burial places surround one of the book's most terrible ironies.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:31.390
Israel had crossed the Jordan under Joshua to enter the land. It had held the fords against Moab under Ehud. Now Israelites used the same crossings to identify and kill fellow Israelites by their speech.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:56.580
The word shibboleth entered modern language as a test of group belonging because of this episode. Its original setting is not a harmless pronunciation game but a massacre. The brief records of Ibzan, Elon and Abdon preserve their family numbers, lengths of service and burial places even though the book tells no extended battles about them.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.380
Chapter 13. The Child Who Would Begin to Save Israel.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:08.230
For forty years the Philistines dominated Israel.

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:19.230
At Zorah lived Manoah of the tribe of Dan. His wife had borne no children. To her—not to Manoah—a messenger of God appeared with a promise.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:37.305
She would conceive and bear a son. She was to avoid wine, strong drink and unclean food. No razor would touch the child's head. He would be a Nazirite devoted to God from the womb and would begin the work of saving Israel from the Philistines.

00:03:37.605 --> 00:03:46.980
She told Manoah about the visitor, whose appearance had seemed like that of a terrifying messenger of God. She had not learned his name.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:58.180
Manoah prayed for him to return and explain the child's upbringing. The messenger came again to the woman as she sat in the field. She ran to bring her husband.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:14.055
Manoah asked what rules would govern the child. The visitor repeated the instructions to his wife. Manoah offered to prepare a young goat. The messenger said he would not eat it, but that a burnt offering should be offered to God.

00:04:14.355 --> 00:04:20.180
When Manoah asked the visitor's name, he was told that it was beyond disclosure.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:29.680
Manoah made the offering upon a rock. As the flame rose, the messenger ascended in it, and the couple fell upon their faces.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:42.005
Manoah feared they would die because they had seen God. His wife offered a calmer conclusion: if God had intended their deaths, why accept the offering and announce a child?

00:04:42.305 --> 00:04:53.255
She bore a son and named him Samson. He grew, God blessed him, and the divine spirit began to move him between Zorah and Eshtaol, in the camp of Dan.

00:04:53.555 --> 00:05:01.155
The boy whose strength would become famous enters the story through the faith and reasoning of an unnamed mother.

00:05:01.955 --> 00:05:22.170
Samson is dedicated as a Nazirite from the womb, and the mother's restrictions are part of that announcement. The messenger says Samson will begin to deliver Israel from Philistines, not complete the work. The woman receives both visits first and reasons more calmly than Manoah after the fire sign.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:27.420
Chapter 14. The Lion, the Honey, and the Riddle.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:34.895
Samson went down to Timnah and saw a Philistine woman whom he wanted to marry.

00:05:35.195 --> 00:05:52.545
His parents protested. Could he find no woman among his own people? Samson insisted that they arrange the match because she pleased him. The narrator says the situation would become an occasion for conflict with the Philistines, who then ruled Israel.

00:05:52.845 --> 00:06:06.270
On the way to Timnah, as they approached its vineyards, a young lion attacked Samson. God's spirit came powerfully upon him, and he tore it apart with his bare hands. He did not tell his parents.

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:19.520
Later, returning to marry the woman, he turned aside to the lion's carcass and discovered bees and honey inside. He ate some and gave some to his parents without telling them its source.

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:38.120
At Timnah he held a feast. Thirty companions were assigned to him. Samson offered them a riddle with a wager: solve it during the seven days of celebration and he would give thirty linen garments and thirty changes of clothing. Fail, and they owed him the same.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:44.270
“Out of the eater came something to eat,” he said, “and out of the strong came sweetness.”

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:52.995
The men could not solve it. They threatened to burn Samson's bride and her father's household unless she obtained the answer.

00:06:53.295 --> 00:07:06.620
She wept before Samson, accusing him of not loving her because he withheld the secret. He had not even told his parents, he replied. Under her pressure he finally told her, and she told the men.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:15.395
Before sunset on the seventh day, the companions answered: what was sweeter than honey, and what stronger than a lion?

00:07:15.695 --> 00:07:23.595
Samson knew they had learned it from his bride. The harsh answer he gave compared her to a heifer used for plowing.

00:07:23.895 --> 00:07:35.145
God's spirit came upon him. He went to Ashkelon, killed thirty men, took their clothing and paid the wager with it. He returned angrily to his father's house.

00:07:35.445 --> 00:07:39.620
His bride was given to the companion who had attended him.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:51.170
A lion's carcass, a secret, a gambling challenge and a threat against a young woman's family had escalated into murder. The marriage had not even begun.

00:07:51.970 --> 00:08:11.835
The lion and honey are not incidental; they supply the riddle's answer. Samson's bride is threatened with death by burning before she reveals it. The men killed at Ashkelon are not described as the men who solved the riddle. Judges 15 carries forward the consequences of the marriage breakdown.

00:08:12.935 --> 00:08:16.435
Chapter 15. The Price of Revenge.

00:08:18.085 --> 00:08:35.610
At wheat harvest Samson returned to Timnah with a young goat, intending to visit his wife. Her father refused him entrance. Believing Samson had rejected her, he had given her to Samson's companion and now offered the younger sister in her place.

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:41.860
Samson announced that he would strike the Philistines and considered himself justified.

00:08:42.160 --> 00:09:02.500
He caught three hundred foxes—or animals of the kind described by the Hebrew word commonly translated “foxes”—paired them tail to tail, attached a torch between each pair and released them into Philistine fields. Standing grain, harvested grain and olive groves burned.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:12.150
The Philistines traced the destruction to Samson and to the Timnite who had given away his wife. They burned the woman and her father to death.

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:18.975
Samson retaliated with another attack, then withdrew to a cleft in the rock of Etam.

00:09:19.275 --> 00:09:31.275
The Philistines marched into Judah and camped at Lehi. The men of Judah asked why. The Philistines said they had come to bind Samson and do to him what he had done to them.

00:09:31.575 --> 00:09:44.300
Three thousand Judahites went to Samson's refuge. Their argument was blunt: did he not know the Philistines ruled them? Why provoke the occupiers and expose everyone else?

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:50.250
Samson answered that he had only done to the Philistines what they had done to him.

00:09:50.550 --> 00:10:05.075
The Judahites intended to hand him over. Samson demanded a promise that they would not kill him themselves. They swore to bind him but leave his death to the Philistines. They tied him with two new ropes and brought him out.

00:10:05.375 --> 00:10:19.975
At Lehi the Philistines shouted in triumph. The spirit of God came upon Samson, and the ropes broke from his arms like burned flax. He found the fresh jawbone of a donkey and struck down a thousand men.

00:10:20.275 --> 00:10:31.975
He celebrated in a short poem playing on the Hebrew words for donkey and heaps of slain men, then threw away the jawbone. The site acquired the name Ramath-lehi.

00:10:32.275 --> 00:10:36.825
Then the strong man was overcome by an ordinary need.

00:10:37.125 --> 00:10:46.500
He was desperately thirsty. He called to God: after such a deliverance, would he die for lack of water and fall into Philistine hands?

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:57.925
God opened a hollow place at Lehi and water came out. Samson drank, recovered his strength and named the spring En-hakkore, the spring of the one who calls.

00:10:58.225 --> 00:11:04.625
The chapter records that he judged Israel during Philistine domination for twenty years.

00:11:04.925 --> 00:11:17.050
It does not say that the violence stopped. Each retaliation had produced another. The conflict was bigger than Samson and much bigger than the broken marriage that had set it off.

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:47.640
The story is a cycle of retaliation, not an uncomplicated act of national liberation. Samson's wife and father-in-law die at Philistine hands; the men of Judah fear the consequences of his actions; and the ropes are broken only after he is delivered to the occupiers. The Hebrew animal term may mean foxes or jackals, depending on interpretation. The chapter explicitly reports both the jawbone and the spring at Lehi.

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:52.490
Chapter 16. The Last Strength of Samson.

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:58.440
Samson entered Gaza and visited a prostitute.

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:14.040
The people of the city learned he was there and watched its gate through the night, planning to kill him in the morning. But at midnight he rose, tore away the city gates with their posts and bar, and carried them toward the hill facing Hebron.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:19.015
Later he loved a woman in the Valley of Sorek named Delilah.

00:12:19.315 --> 00:12:29.940
The Philistine rulers offered her eleven hundred pieces of silver from each of them if she could discover the secret of Samson's strength and how to bind him.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:32.615
She asked him directly.

00:12:32.915 --> 00:12:47.265
First he claimed that seven fresh bowstrings would make him as weak as another man. She tied him with them, while Philistine men lay in wait, and cried that the enemy was upon him. Samson broke the cords.

00:12:47.565 --> 00:12:52.515
Next he named new ropes never used for work. Those broke too.

00:12:52.815 --> 00:13:02.740
Third, he suggested weaving the seven locks of his hair into a loom. She fastened his hair with a pin; he woke and pulled the equipment free.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:23.255
Delilah accused him of withholding his heart despite professing love. She pressed him day after day until he told her the truth of his dedication: no razor had touched his head because he was a Nazirite from his mother's womb. If his hair were shaved, his strength would leave him.

00:13:23.555 --> 00:13:34.555
Delilah summoned the Philistine rulers, who brought the money. She made Samson sleep in her lap and called for a man to shave his seven locks. His strength departed.

00:13:34.855 --> 00:13:38.005
“The Philistines are upon you,” she said.

00:13:38.305 --> 00:13:47.830
Samson woke expecting to escape as before. The narrator adds the devastating fact: he did not know God had departed from him.

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:57.030
The Philistines captured him, blinded him and brought him to Gaza. Bound with bronze chains, he ground grain in prison.

00:13:57.330 --> 00:14:00.080
His hair began to grow again.

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:22.220
At a feast honoring their god Dagon, the Philistine rulers celebrated the capture of their enemy. They brought Samson from prison to entertain the crowd and placed him between the supporting pillars of a crowded building. About three thousand men and women watched from the roof, and the leading Philistine officials were present.

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:29.720
Samson asked the young man guiding him to let him feel the pillars so that he could lean against them.

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:37.595
Then he called on God to remember him and strengthen him just once more, so that he could avenge his two eyes.

00:14:37.895 --> 00:14:43.995
He braced himself between the central pillars and said, “Let me die with the Philistines.”

00:14:44.295 --> 00:14:52.495
The building collapsed. The narrative says more people died in that destruction than Samson had killed throughout his life.

00:14:52.795 --> 00:15:03.595
His brothers and father's household recovered his body and buried him between Zorah and Eshtaol, in the tomb of Manoah. He had judged Israel for twenty years.

00:15:03.895 --> 00:15:13.145
Samson had been dedicated to God before his birth. His final act was a prayer and a collapse that killed him with his enemies.

00:15:13.445 --> 00:15:22.645
He began the work of resistance against Philistine rule, as the messenger had promised. The book does not claim that he ended it.

00:15:23.445 --> 00:15:48.110
Delilah tries three false methods before hearing about Samson's hair. The text makes clear that the power is bound up with his Nazirite dedication and divine presence, not a mechanical property of hair alone. It says his hair grew again but does not state that the growth itself restored his strength. His final prayer asks explicitly for vengeance for his eyes.

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:52.635
Chapter 17. The Priest for Hire.

00:15:54.285 --> 00:15:58.435
There was a man in Ephraim's hill country named Micah.

00:15:58.735 --> 00:16:10.810
He confessed to his mother that he had stolen eleven hundred pieces of silver from her—the very money over which she had pronounced a curse. She answered with a blessing upon her son.

00:16:11.110 --> 00:16:26.035
Micah returned the silver. His mother announced that she had dedicated it to God for him, to make an image. She took two hundred pieces and gave them to a metalworker, who made a carved and molten idol for Micah's house.

00:16:26.335 --> 00:16:34.535
Micah already had a private shrine. He made an ephod and household gods and installed one of his sons as priest.

00:16:34.835 --> 00:16:45.485
The narrator pauses to state the condition of the country: in those days there was no king in Israel, and everyone did what seemed right in his own eyes.

00:16:45.785 --> 00:16:53.860
Then a young Levite from Bethlehem in Judah set out seeking a place to live and work. He came to Micah's house.

00:16:54.160 --> 00:17:01.335
Micah asked where he had come from. The Levite explained that he was traveling to find a place to settle.

00:17:01.635 --> 00:17:17.535
Micah offered employment: ten pieces of silver each year, clothing, food and a place in the household. The Levite would become Micah's priest and spiritual father. The young man agreed and was treated like one of Micah's sons.

00:17:17.835 --> 00:17:20.560
Micah formally installed him.

00:17:20.860 --> 00:17:27.535
Now, he declared, he knew God would favor him because he had a Levite serving as his priest.

00:17:27.835 --> 00:17:30.735
It is a quietly alarming story.

00:17:31.035 --> 00:17:46.510
A man steals silver from his mother, returns it, uses part of it to create a forbidden image, builds a domestic shrine and hires religious legitimacy. He believes the arrangement has secured divine blessing.

00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:56.935
The narrator never endorses Micah's conclusion. Instead the refrain about everyone following his own judgment hangs over the household.

00:17:57.235 --> 00:18:05.435
A Levite has been bought for wages, and the religious authority he supposedly represents is about to be bought again.

00:18:06.235 --> 00:18:27.000
Micah's mother speaks in the name of Israel's God while commissioning an image. That contradiction is central. The hired Levite's promise of legitimacy is Micah's belief, not the narrator's approval. The phrase “everyone did what was right in his own eyes” first appears here and will frame the book's conclusion.

00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:31.575
Chapter 18. The Tribe That Took a God.

00:18:33.225 --> 00:18:39.850
Dan had been allotted land, but the tribe still struggled to occupy a secure inheritance.

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:59.215
Five men from Zorah and Eshtaol were sent to search for territory. Passing through Ephraim's hills, they stayed near Micah's house and recognized the voice of his hired Levite. They asked who had brought him there and what he was doing. He told them that Micah employed him as priest.

00:18:59.515 --> 00:19:06.090
They asked him to seek God's guidance for their expedition. He assured them they could go in peace.

00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:20.515
The scouts continued to Laish and found an isolated community living quietly, far from Sidonian support and without close alliances. The land was rich, they reported to their tribe. Why delay?

00:19:20.815 --> 00:19:30.465
Six hundred armed men set out from Dan, camped near Kiriath-jearim at the place called Mahaneh-dan, and traveled north through Ephraim.

00:19:30.765 --> 00:19:38.915
At Micah's house, the five scouts told the soldiers about the ephod, household gods and images inside.

00:19:39.215 --> 00:19:54.240
The armed men stood at the entrance while the scouts took Micah's religious objects. The Levite asked what they were doing. The Danites answered with an offer: was it better to serve as priest for one household or an entire tribe?

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:58.990
The Levite was pleased. He took the objects and joined them.

00:19:59.290 --> 00:20:10.790
Micah pursued with neighbors. The Danites asked why he was coming. He replied that they had taken the god he had made and the priest he employed; what did he have left?

00:20:11.090 --> 00:20:17.765
They threatened him with violence if he continued. Seeing that they were stronger, Micah went home.

00:20:18.065 --> 00:20:31.090
The Danites arrived at Laish, killed its inhabitants and burned the city. No ally came to rescue the isolated community. They rebuilt it and called it Dan after their ancestor.

00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:54.280
There they set up Micah's image. A priest named Jonathan, descended from Gershom, is identified in the transmitted text with a genealogy containing a scribal peculiarity traditionally discussed by commentators. He and his descendants served in the sanctuary. The image remained while the house of God was at Shiloh.

00:20:54.580 --> 00:21:06.055
The tribe had solved its territorial problem by taking a peaceful town and had furnished its new religious center by stealing an idol and hiring away a priest.

00:21:06.355 --> 00:21:15.180
The narrator has now shown an entire tribe doing what Micah did in one house: taking possession and calling it devotion.

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:48.970
The five scouts, six hundred armed men, theft of the image, hiring of the Levite and destruction of Laish are separate, indispensable acts. Judges 18:30 contains a well-known Masoretic textual feature in the priest's ancestral name, often discussed in Jewish commentary. We should not invent certainty about the genealogy from a modernized spelling. The conquest of Laish is not portrayed as a battle against the Philistines or the enemies who had kept Dan from its original land.

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:53.395
Chapter 19. The Doorway at Gibeah.

00:21:55.045 --> 00:22:03.720
In a time without a king, a Levite living in Ephraim's remote hill country had a concubine from Bethlehem in Judah.

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:18.270
The text says she was unfaithful to him and returned to her father's house, where she remained four months. The Levite went after her to speak kindly and bring her back. He traveled with a servant and two donkeys.

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:33.220
Her father welcomed him and kept the visitors for three days of food and drink. On the fourth and fifth days he repeatedly urged them to stay. Finally, late in the fifth day, the Levite insisted on departing.

00:22:33.520 --> 00:22:54.010
As evening approached, they came near Jebus, the city later known as Jerusalem. The servant suggested lodging there. His master refused because the inhabitants were not Israelites. They continued toward Gibeah or Ramah, and sunset caught them near Gibeah in Benjamin's territory.

00:22:54.310 --> 00:22:58.710
They sat in the town square. No one offered hospitality.

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:12.185
At last an older man returning from fieldwork invited them home. He was himself from Ephraim, living among the Benjaminites. He offered food, shelter and fodder for the animals.

00:23:12.485 --> 00:23:21.035
As the household ate, men of the city surrounded the house and demanded that the visitor be brought out for sexual assault.

00:23:21.335 --> 00:23:32.535
The host pleaded with them not to commit such an outrage against a guest. Then he made an appalling offer: his own unmarried daughter and the Levite's concubine.

00:23:32.835 --> 00:23:35.360
The men refused to listen.

00:23:35.660 --> 00:23:44.735
The Levite thrust his concubine outside to them. They sexually assaulted and abused her through the night, releasing her toward morning.

00:23:45.035 --> 00:23:50.660
At dawn she reached the house and fell at the doorway, her hands on the threshold.

00:23:50.960 --> 00:24:00.760
In the morning the Levite opened the door and told her to get up so they could leave. She did not answer. He put her on the donkey and returned to his home.

00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:06.210
There he cut her body into twelve pieces and sent them throughout Israel.

00:24:06.510 --> 00:24:16.310
The people who received the pieces said nothing like this had been seen since the Exodus. They called for deliberation, counsel and a response.

00:24:16.610 --> 00:24:31.960
The chapter's horror does not rest only in the crimes of Gibeah. A host offers women to protect a male guest, a Levite hands a woman to attackers, and afterward her remains become the means of a national summons.

00:24:32.260 --> 00:24:39.510
The woman never receives a name in the book. The next chapter will report how the nation answered her death.

00:24:40.310 --> 00:25:11.450
This chapter describes sexual violence and its aftermath. A responsible literary retelling should neither sensationalize it nor omit the Levite's action in giving the woman to the mob. The narrator does not state unambiguously at which precise moment she died; the subsequent assembly treats her as murdered. The scene recalls the threat against visitors in Genesis 19, but that comparison is a literary cross-reference, not a newly invented event.

00:25:12.550 --> 00:25:16.500
Chapter 20. Israel Goes to War with Itself.

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:32.175
The twelve pieces sent across Israel brought men together from Dan to Beersheba and from Gilead, assembled before God at Mizpah. The text gives four hundred thousand armed men among the gathering.

00:25:32.475 --> 00:25:47.475
The Levite recounted the violence at Gibeah. His account emphasized the men's attack and the death of his concubine; readers who recall the previous chapter will notice what he did not describe about his own conduct.

00:25:47.775 --> 00:25:57.500
The assembly resolved to act against the town. It organized supplies for the army and demanded that Benjamin surrender the perpetrators.

00:25:57.800 --> 00:25:59.975
Benjamin refused.

00:26:00.275 --> 00:26:20.065
Instead, the tribe gathered twenty-six thousand fighting men from its towns and seven hundred selected men from Gibeah. Among its warriors were seven hundred left-handed slingers, men capable of striking a hair-width target. The other Israelites still vastly outnumbered them.

00:26:20.365 --> 00:26:25.265
Israel asked God which tribe should go first. Judah was named.

00:26:25.565 --> 00:26:33.890
The first battle at Gibeah was a catastrophe for the attackers. Benjamin killed twenty-two thousand Israelites.

00:26:34.190 --> 00:26:42.265
They regrouped, wept before God and asked whether they should fight their brother Benjamin again. They were told to go.

00:26:42.565 --> 00:26:47.015
The second battle killed another eighteen thousand Israelites.

00:26:47.315 --> 00:27:05.790
Now the people gathered at Bethel, fasted, wept, offered sacrifices and again asked God's counsel. The Ark was there at that time, and Phinehas son of Eleazar, son of Aaron, ministered before it. They were told to fight again; this time victory was promised.

00:27:06.090 --> 00:27:09.265
Israel placed an ambush around Gibeah.

00:27:09.565 --> 00:27:22.790
On the third day its troops approached as before, then drew the Benjaminites away from the city. Benjamin believed it was winning again. About thirty Israelites fell as the defenders pursued.

00:27:23.090 --> 00:27:35.765
But the ambushers struck Gibeah and set the city alight. Smoke rose as the signal. The fleeing Israelite force turned back, and the Benjaminites saw their town burning behind them.

00:27:36.065 --> 00:27:38.515
The battle became a rout.

00:27:38.815 --> 00:27:58.780
The chapter supplies overlapping accounts and figures, including a total of twenty-five thousand one hundred Benjaminite fighting men killed and a further summary near twenty-five thousand. The survivors fled toward the wilderness and the Rock of Rimmon, pursued through the roads toward Gidom.

00:27:59.080 --> 00:28:05.080
Only six hundred Benjaminite men escaped to Rimmon, where they remained four months.

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:14.280
The Israelites then turned against Benjamin's towns, killing inhabitants and livestock and burning the settlements they found.

00:28:14.580 --> 00:28:22.180
What had begun as a response to a brutal crime in Gibeah became the near destruction of an entire tribe.

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:31.380
And the surviving Israelites would soon discover that their vows and their violence had left them with another disaster to solve.

00:28:32.180 --> 00:28:59.520
The Levite's testimony in chapter 20 is not identical to the scene narrated in chapter 19. The two early defeats, the inquiry before the Ark with Phinehas present, and the third-day ambush must all be retained. The battle figures are difficult to reconcile at every point; rather than manufacture arithmetic certainty, the retelling preserves the source's principal totals and signals the overlap.

00:29:00.620 --> 00:29:05.370
Chapter 21. Everyone Did What Was Right in His Own Eyes.

00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:13.495
The civil war left Benjamin reduced to six hundred men hiding at the Rock of Rimmon.

00:29:13.795 --> 00:29:27.345
Before the fighting, the Israelites at Mizpah had sworn that none would give a daughter in marriage to a Benjaminite. Now they mourned the possible extinction of a tribe and asked how it could survive.

00:29:27.645 --> 00:29:32.470
At Bethel they wept, built an altar and offered sacrifices.

00:29:32.770 --> 00:29:46.545
They also remembered another oath: anyone who had failed to appear at the national assembly would be put to death. When the people checked the muster, they discovered that no one had come from Jabesh-gilead.

00:29:46.845 --> 00:29:49.470
Their solution was horrifying.

00:29:49.770 --> 00:30:02.670
Twelve thousand soldiers were sent to destroy Jabesh-gilead. They killed its men, its children and women who had been married. They brought back four hundred young unmarried women to Shiloh.

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:14.320
The assembly proclaimed peace to the six hundred Benjaminites at Rimmon and gave them those four hundred captives. There were still not enough women for the surviving men.

00:30:14.620 --> 00:30:20.645
The elders sought another way without breaking the oath not to give their own daughters.

00:30:20.945 --> 00:30:40.185
They knew of an annual festival at Shiloh, north of Bethel, east of the road to Shechem and south of Lebonah. They instructed the remaining Benjaminites to hide in the vineyards. When the young women of Shiloh came out to dance, the men were to seize wives and return to Benjamin.

00:30:40.485 --> 00:30:50.985
If the women's families protested, the elders would argue that they had not formally given their daughters away, so they had not technically violated the oath.

00:30:51.285 --> 00:31:05.835
The Benjaminites did as they were told. They abducted women from the festival, returned to their assigned territory and rebuilt their towns. The rest of Israel dispersed to its separate tribal inheritances.

00:31:06.135 --> 00:31:11.235
Then the book ends with the same verdict that had appeared in the story of Micah.

00:31:11.535 --> 00:31:17.985
“In those days there was no king in Israel. Everyone did what was right in his own eyes.”

00:31:18.285 --> 00:31:20.810
It is not a happy ending.

00:31:21.110 --> 00:31:41.225
Israel had punished a crime with civil war, nearly destroyed a tribe, murdered the inhabitants of another town and arranged further abductions to preserve the tribe it had devastated. The people had clung to the wording of their oaths while ignoring the moral catastrophe of their choices.

00:31:41.525 --> 00:31:46.675
Joshua had died after calling the nation to choose whom it would serve.

00:31:46.975 --> 00:31:54.200
Judges ends with a nation making choices—and almost no one able to stop the consequences.

00:31:55.000 --> 00:32:22.615
The women of Jabesh-gilead and Shiloh are not described as freely choosing these marriages. The chapter presents killing and abduction as the assembly's solutions. The final refrain is a judgment on the period's disorder; it is not permission to call those acts morally good. This closing sequence demands care in voice performance: restraint, clarity, and no sensational music or dramatic embellishment.
